  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 2.2. Increased capacity of the member States to apply international labour standards</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">CEACR Observation on the application of the Right to Organise and Collective Bargaining Convention, 1949 (No. 98) by Montenegro: &quot;The Committee requested the Government to pursue its efforts to amend the legislation so as to ensure the provision of sufficiently dissuasive sanctions for acts of anti-union discrimination against union members and officials. The Committee notes the Government&apos;s indication that: (i) section 189 of the new Labour Law prescribes voluntary membership in a trade union or employers&apos; association and stipulates that no one can be placed in a less favourable position due to membership in such organizations and participation or failure to participate in their activities; (ii) section 7 prohibits direct and indirect discrimination against persons seeking employment, as well as employees, on the ground of, among others, trade union membership etc. It further notes that section 173(5) stipulates that acting as a representative of employees in line with the law does not constitute a justified reason for termination of employment, that section 196 provides protection against anti-union discrimination of trade union representatives during the mandate, as well as six months after its termination, and that section 180(5) stipulates the possibility of reinstatement and compensation in case of illegal dismissal. The Committee notes with satisfaction the adoption of the above provisions.&quot; The ILO has provided extensive evidence-based legal and technical advice, as well as exposure to European comparative law and practice to the tripartite working group (WG), which drafted the amendments to the Labour Law.

The ILO has also facilitated tripartite negotiations over possible legal solutions during several meetings of the tripartite WG. According to the information received from the Ministry of Labour, over 60 per cent of the ILO&apos;s technical comments to the draft Labour Law were incorporated in the final text as adopted by Parliament.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">2.2.1. Number of cases of progress in the application of ratified Conventions noted with satisfaction by the supervisory bodies</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 2.3. Increased capacity of the ILO constituents to engage in a forward-looking international labour standards policy</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tripartite constituents  actively participated  in the implementation of relevant ILS through genuine engagement in negotiating and drafting the new Labour Code, and special laws  in the framework of institutionalised tripartite social dialogue.
ILO comments on the draft Law on Amicable Settlement of Labour Disputes were taken on board by the Ministry of Labour and Social Welfare in proportion of 85%. The law awaits adoption. With the support of the European Union, the ILO provided technical comments on the Montenegrin Labour legislation (Labour Law and Law on Amicable Settlement of Labour Disputes). The ILO facilitated tripartite validation discussions and provided technical support in the implementation of the recommendations by ILO.
The ILO conducted an analysis of possible legal and enforcement gaps with regard to ILO Convention 154 and labour standards, followed by a tripartite validation discussion. 
Technical comments on the Law on Employment of PWD Commentary on the LC.
Montenegrin judges were trained on a set of selected topics, which resulted in inclusion of ILS in the regular training curricula of the Judicial Training Center, and, more importantly, in quoting ILS in the decisions of the Montenegrin courts.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">2.3.2 Number of member States with tripartite mechanisms enabling constituents to effectively engage in the implementation of international labour standards at the national level, including reporting to the supervisory bodies</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
